# AKS Networking — Azure CNI vs Calico vs Istio

## 1. Complete AKS Networking Picture

```text
                         INTERNET
                            |
                            v
                  Azure Load Balancer
                            |
                            v
                     Ingress Controller
                            |
                    frontend Service
                            |
                       kube-proxy
                            |
                            v
                  +------------------+
                  | Frontend Pod     |
                  | 10.10.1.10       |
                  +------------------+
                            |
                            | HTTP request
                            v
                  backend Service
                  10.10.20.100:80
                            |
                       kube-proxy
                            |
                            v
                  +------------------+
                  | Backend Pod      |
                  | 10.10.2.20       |
                  +------------------+
```

---

## 2. CoreDNS

Suppose the application uses:

```text
http://backend-service:8080
```

The Pod doesn't know the IP address of `backend-service`.

It asks DNS:

```text
Frontend Pod
     |
     | "What is backend-service?"
     v
   CoreDNS
     |
     | 10.10.20.100
     v
Frontend Pod
```

### CoreDNS responsibility

> **CoreDNS = Service name → Service IP/name resolution**

CoreDNS does **not** send the actual application traffic to the backend.

---

## 3. kube-proxy

After DNS resolution, the frontend knows:

```text
backend-service = 10.10.20.100
```

The application sends:

```text
Frontend Pod
      |
      | destination: 10.10.20.100:8080
      v
backend Service
```

The Service IP is a **virtual IP**.

`kube-proxy` programs the node networking rules so traffic can be redirected/load-balanced to an actual backend Pod.

```text
                 Service
              10.10.20.100
                    |
              kube-proxy rules
                    |
             +------+------+
             |             |
         Backend Pod    Backend Pod
         10.10.2.20     10.10.3.25
```

### kube-proxy responsibility

> **kube-proxy = Service IP → Backend Pod**

---

## 4. Azure CNI

Azure CNI is responsible for the underlying **Pod networking**.

Example:

```text
Node 1
  |
  +-- Frontend Pod
       IP: 10.10.1.10

Node 2
  |
  +-- Backend Pod
       IP: 10.10.2.20
```

Conceptually:

```text
Frontend Pod
10.10.1.10
     |
     | Azure CNI
     |
Azure VNet
     |
     | Azure networking
     |
Backend Pod
10.10.2.20
```

With **Azure CNI Overlay**, the exact IP architecture is different, but the important concept is:

> **CNI provides the underlying Pod networking.**

---

## 5. Calico

Calico can provide **network policy enforcement** and, depending on the AKS configuration, networking functionality.

For example:

```text
Frontend
   |
   | ALLOW
   v
Backend
```

But:

```text
Frontend
   |
   | DENY
   X
Database
```

You can define a Kubernetes `NetworkPolicy`:

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-frontend
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: backend
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: frontend
```

This means:

```text
Frontend Pod
     |
     | ALLOW
     v
Backend Pod

Other Pod
     |
     | DENY
     X
Backend Pod
```

### Calico responsibility

> **Calico = Network connectivity/policy, depending on configuration**

In AKS, the exact Calico role depends on the selected networking and network-policy configuration.

---

## 6. Istio

Istio is a **service mesh**.

It operates at the **application/service communication layer**.

Example:

```text
Frontend Pod
     |
     v
+-------------+
| Envoy Proxy |
+-------------+
     |
     | mTLS / HTTP
     | Retry
     | Timeout
     | Traffic routing
     v
+-------------+
| Envoy Proxy |
+-------------+
     |
     v
Backend Pod
```

Istio can provide:

* mTLS
* Retries
* Timeouts
* Traffic routing
* Traffic splitting
* Circuit breaking
* Authorization
* Observability
* Distributed tracing

### Istio responsibility

> **Istio = Application/service-to-service traffic management**

---

